\section{Prediction}{

The simulation produces a value \emph{exactly} at each requested \code{batch_nr} (no
interpolation). Consecutive requested \code{batch_nr}s define the simulation's
per-step batch size: a request from \code{b_prev} to \code{b_next} is realized as one
optimizer proposal batch of size \code{b_next - b_prev}. When the requested
spacing varies, the optimizer's \code{batch_size} is adjusted and the optimizer
is run again. This matches the recorded curve when the originating run used
\code{batch_size = 1} (so each \code{batch_nr} step is one evaluation) or when the
requested spacing matches the originating batch sizes.
}

\section{Prediction types}{

Each restart is an independent forward simulation, i.e. one joint sample path
of the \emph{realised} trajectory over the requested batches. The \code{response} is the
across-restart mean on the \link{lce_link} scale, back-transformed (the predictive
median under the link). The total predictive \code{se} is the across-restart
standard deviation on the link scale (\code{0} with a single restart); the
epistemic \code{se_epistemic} is that divided by \code{sqrt(n_restarts)} (the standard
error of the expected curve). The \code{samples} predict type returns the restart
paths themselves (one column per restart), \code{quantiles} their per-row sample
quantiles, and \code{target_reached} the fraction of restart paths that have
reached the target. Richer-than-\code{response} predict types therefore need
\code{n_restarts > 1} (with a stochastic proposer).
}

